# A module attribute read through its qualifier, while the program ALSO binds a
# variable of that same name — `digits = string.digits` is the ordinary Python
# spelling of an import and it did not compile: "undefined variable (digits)",
# reported against the very line creating the variable.
#
# Four assertions, because "it compiles" is not the property. The bare name must
# still read the PROGRAM's value (an unqualified name is not the module's — that
# scoping rule is what the guard being relaxed here exists for), the qualified
# name must read the MODULE's, and the collision case must produce the module's
# value rather than the program's previous one. A fix that let the qualifier see
# through to the shim but also let it lose to the local would pass a compile
# check and fail line 2.
#
# Expectations are CPython's own output.
# bug-n-assigning-to-a-name-that-collides-with-a-pascal-shim-attribute-fails
import string

digits = "xyz"
print(digits)
print(string.digits)
digits = string.digits
print(digits)
print(len(string.whitespace))
